        "<strong>Baraʼ (<em>בָּרָא</em>) and the LXX choice.</strong> The Hebrew verb <em>baraʼ</em> in the Hebrew Bible takes only God as subject, never humans. The Septuagint renders it not with <em>ktiżeīn</em> (“to found / institute,” the verb used elsewhere for divine creation), but with <em>epoīēsen</em> (“made”, ἐποίησεν) — a more ordinary verb. The shift narrows the theological reach of the Hebrew. Genesis Rabbah 1:1 reads <em>berešit</em> as <em>be-rešit</em>, “by means of [wisdom]”, citing Proverbs 8:22; Philo similarly identifies the Logos as the instrument of creation.",
