    (
        1,
        14,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;Let there be lights in the firmament... for signs, and for seasons, and for days, and years&#x27; Ephrem makes the foundational anti-astrological argument: the heavenly luminaries are SERVANTS for marking time, not GODS for determining destiny. The fourfold purpose (signs / seasons / days / years) Ephrem reads as exhausting the legitimate functions of the heavens; any further attribution (horoscopic prediction, fate, divinatory power) is human invention not divine grant. The Tewahedo liturgical calendar — which depends on careful astronomical observation for determining Easter (the Bahire Hassab computus tradition) — derives its theological legitimacy directly from this Ephremic reading: astronomy in service of liturgy is sacred; astrology in service of fate-prediction is forbidden.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis I.14 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
    ),
