        "Be fruitful, and multiply",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>First divine blessing.</strong> The first explicit blessing in Scripture (Hebrew <em>peru u-revu</em>, <em>פְּרוּ</em> <em>וּרְבוּ</em>) is given to sea creatures and birds, then repeated to humanity in 1:28. Rabbinic tradition derives the obligation of procreation from this verse (b. Yevamot 65b), debating whether it applies to women as well as men. The blessing’s grammatical form is intensive — both verbs in the imperative — suggesting not merely permission but mandate. The motif structures the patriarchal narratives, where procreation against barrenness becomes the locus of divine action (Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel, Hannah).",
        "User original",
