        "word",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Gan / paradēisos (παράδεισος).</strong> The LXX renders <em>gan</em> (“garden”) with the Persian loan-word <em>paradēisos</em>, an enclosed royal park. From the Greek Bible the term entered Latin (<em>paradisus</em>) and became, in early Jewish and Christian usage, a name for the heavenly garden of the righteous (cf. Luke 23:43, 2 Cor 12:4, Rev 2:7). The Hebrew <em>ʻeden</em> may relate to Akkadian <em>edinu</em> (“plain”) or the Hebrew root for “delight”; the second etymology drives much rabbinic wordplay.",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
