        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The serpent.</strong> Genesis 3 calls it simply <em>nahash</em> (snake), not Satan. The identification of the serpent with Satan is a later development first explicit in the intertestamental literature (Wisdom of Solomon 2:24) and the New Testament (Revelation 12:9). In the original Hebrew narrative, the serpent is a clever talking creature, more cunning than other animals — not yet the cosmic adversary of later tradition.",
        "User original",
