        "in the cool of the day",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>God walking.</strong> The image of God “walking” (Hebrew <em>mithallek</em>, <em>מִתְהַלֵּךְ</em>) in the garden suggests a form of divine immanence not preserved in later strata of biblical theology. The same verb describes God’s presence in the wilderness camp (Lev 26:12; Deut 23:14) and the “walking” of Enoch and Noah (Gen 5:22, 6:9 — both “walked with God”). Ancient interpreters were uncomfortable with the anthropomorphism; <em>Targum Onkelos</em> paraphrases as “the voice of the Word of the Lord” walking, removing the embodied imagery and substituting a hypostatized divine speech.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum",
