        "cursed above all livestock, and above every animal of the field",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Curse on the serpent.</strong> The serpent receives the only direct curse in the Eden narrative — neither the woman nor Adam is cursed; only the ground and the serpent. The serpent’s “going on its belly” and “eating dust” are read by some commentators as etiological explanation of snake locomotion; by others as theological reduction — the serpent is demoted from upright/standing creature to permanent humiliation. Isaiah 65:25 picks up the imagery in its eschatological vision (“dust shall be the serpent’s food”), suggesting the curse persists into the new creation rather than being reversed.",
        "User original",
