        "five hundred years old",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Noah’s late paternity.</strong> Noah’s becoming a father at 500 is anomalous in the chronology — all previous patriarchs become fathers between 65 (Mahalalel, Enoch) and 187 (Methuselah). The delay marks Noah as set apart. Rabbinic interpretation (<em>Bereshit Rabbah</em> 26:2) reads it as divine reservation: Noah’s children are deliberately delayed so they will be young when the flood comes (Shem, Ham, Japheth are still relatively young — under 100 — when they enter the ark). The chronological pattern thus prepares the narrative pivot from primordial history to flood narrative.",
        "User original",
