        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — 1 Enoch (Ethiopian tradition)",
        "1 Enoch (Ethiopian tradition) (-200).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>1 Enoch (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Book of the Watchers (1 Enoch 6-11)</em> <small>(c. 200 BC)</small><p>Where the Hebrew Bible&#x27;s &#x27;sons of God&#x27; (b&#x27;nei ha&#x27;elohim) saw the daughters of men, the Ethiopian Tewahedo tradition reads through 1 Enoch&#x27;s expansion: the Watchers, two hundred angels under Semjâzâ, descend on Mount Hermon and bind themselves by oath to take human wives. The corruption is not human alone but angelic-human, requiring the Flood as a cosmic — not merely punitive — purgation. The Tewahedo canon is the only Christian canon to receive 1 Enoch as Scripture, making this reading load-bearing for its theology of evil.</p></aside>',
        "1 Enoch 6:1-8, trans. R.H. Charles, The Book of Enoch (Oxford: Clarendon, 1912). PD. The Ethiopic Mäṣḥafä Hēnok preserves the only complete text.",
