        "one hundred twenty years",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>One hundred twenty years.</strong> The verse is interpretively contested. Two readings dominate: (1) shortened human lifespan — humans now limited to 120 years; problematic given Noah’s 950, Abraham’s 175. (2) Probationary period — 120 years of divine forbearance before the flood; favored by most modern commentators (Cassuto, Sarna, Wenham) and by traditional Jewish exegesis (Rashi, Ibn Ezra). The number itself recurs as Moses’s lifespan (Deut 34:7), suggesting a span of completed earthly life under prophetic dispensation.",
        "User paraphrase; references Rashi, Ibn Ezra; summarises Sarna, Wenham",
