        "every imagination of the thoughts of man",
        "comm-reformation",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Inclination of the heart.</strong> The Hebrew <em>yetzer</em> (<em>יֵצֶר</em>, “inclination, formation”) shares its root with <em>yatzar</em> (“to form, fashion” — used of God forming Adam in 2:7). Rabbinic theology developed this into the doctrine of the two <em>yetzarim</em>: the good inclination (<em>yetzer ha-tov</em>) and the evil inclination (<em>yetzer ha-ra</em>) coexisting in every person (b. Berakhot 61a). Augustinian and later Calvinist doctrines of total depravity cite this verse as foundational. The same yetzer-language reappears at 8:21 after the flood — the diagnosis is unchanged by the deluge, an unsettling continuity.",
