        "",
        "Yahweh was sorry that he had made man",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Divine regret.</strong> The Hebrew verb <em>nicham</em> (<em>נִחַם</em>) ranges from “regret” through “be moved to pity” to “change one’s mind.” The classical theological tension: how can the immutable God “regret”? Targum Onkelos paraphrases as “the Lord said in His Word” — softening the anthropomorphism by attributing it to the divine speech rather than the divine being itself. Patristic readings (Augustine, <em>City of God</em> XV.25) treat the regret as accommodated to human comprehension. Process theology and open theism take it more literally as evidence of genuine divine responsiveness to creaturely freedom.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum, Augustine",
