        "I will establish my covenant with you",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The first covenant.</strong> This is the first occurrence of <em>berit</em> (<em>בְּרִית</em>, “covenant”) in the Bible, formally enacted in 9:9–17. The Noahide covenant is universal — with all flesh — not particular to a people. Rabbinic theology derives the seven Noahide commandments (<em>sheva mitzvot Bnei Noach</em>) from this passage and from the post-flood covenant of ch 9, treating them as the moral law binding on all humanity (b. Sanhedrin 56a). Christian theology has used the Noahide covenant as a model for natural law accessible to all peoples regardless of revelation.",
        "User original",
