        "It went back and forth, until the waters were dried up",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Raven and dove.</strong> The raven returns nothing (it eats carrion and would survive on floating corpses); the dove returns with hope (the olive leaf). Christian tradition has read the dove as type of the Holy Spirit (descending at Jesus’s baptism, Mark 1:10) and the olive branch as symbol of peace and reconciliation. The raven-dove contrast was developed in patristic exegesis (Augustine, <em>Contra Faustum</em> 12.20) as type of the unrepentant versus the faithful — the raven did not return because it was content with the carrion of the old world.",
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine",
