    (
        9,
        13,
        "p",
        "I set my rainbow in the cloud",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Qeshet — God hangs up his war-bow",
        '<strong>The rainbow as hung-up war-bow.</strong> Hebrew <em>qeshet</em> means primarily “bow” — the weapon of war (Gen 21:20; 27:3; Ps 7:12). Only in this passage and Ezekiel 1:28 does it refer to the meteorological rainbow. Patristic and rabbinic interpretation read the placement of the bow “in the cloud” as God hanging up his weapon, with its string toward the earth — a permanent gesture of divine restraint after the flood-judgment. ANE parallels: Mesopotamian deities are routinely depicted with bows (Marduk, Ishtar). The Genesis text, by appropriating war-bow vocabulary for a peace-sign, makes a polemical point: the divine warrior has put down his weapon. <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b86-c4">Revelation 4:3</a> places a rainbow around the throne — covenantal continuity in the heavenly courtroom.',
        "User original",
    ),
