    (
        9,
        25,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>Noah&#x27;s pronouncement after the Ham-incident — &#x27;Cursed be Canaan; a servant of servants shall he be unto his brethren&#x27; — Ephrem reads with careful precision against any reading that would extract a permanent racial-ethnic curse. The curse falls on CANAAN (Ham&#x27;s son), not on Ham himself or on Ham&#x27;s other descendants; the cursing is therefore localized and historical (fulfilled in the Canaanite-Israelite encounters of Joshua-Judges), not racially universal. Against the early-modern American mis-application of this verse to justify African slavery, Ephrem&#x27;s text-bounded reading clearly precludes such extension: the curse is on a specific lineage (Canaan), for a specific reason (Canaan&#x27;s prefigured idolatry and immorality), with a specific fulfillment (the dispossession of the Canaanite peoples by Israel). The Tewahedo reading inherits Ephrem&#x27;s text-bounded precision.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis IX.25 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
    ),
    (
        9,
        25,
        "m",
        "Canaan is cursed",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The curse of Canaan.</strong> One of the most exegetically and historically loaded texts in the Bible. The curse falls on Canaan (Ham’s son), not on Ham who actually saw Noah’s nakedness — a textual peculiarity with multiple proposed solutions (Ham’s descendants pre-determined to inherit; Canaan implicated in his father’s act; later editorial framing for the conquest narrative). Misused throughout Christian history, especially in defending African chattel slavery, by misidentifying Ham’s descendants. Modern critical reading sees the passage as etiology for the Israelite-Canaanite conflict — explaining why Israel would later dispossess Canaan.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        25,
        "p",
        "Canaan is cursed",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Misuse of the curse of Canaan",
        "<strong>The historical misuse of this curse.</strong> Through medieval and especially American antebellum interpretation, this verse was weaponized to justify African enslavement — the “curse of Ham” (transferred from Canaan to Ham, then to Africa, then to all dark-skinned peoples). Every step of that exegetical chain is wrong. The curse falls on <em>Canaan</em>, not Ham; “Canaan” in Genesis = the Levantine peoples (10:15–19), whose territory was later possessed by Israel — i.e., the curse is fulfilled by the conquest of Canaan, not by chattel slavery centuries later. Modern Christian and Jewish scholarship is unanimous that the verse cannot bear the racialist weight imposed on it. The curse is geographically and ethnically specific; it has no implications for African peoples or for the legitimacy of human enslavement in any form.",
        "User original",
    ),
