    (
        10,
        5,
        "",
        "islands of the nations divided",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Table of Nations as world-map.</strong> Genesis 10 is the most ambitious geographical-ethnographic catalogue in the ancient world before Greek writers like Hecataeus and Herodotus. The seventy nations descended from Noah’s three sons map the world known to ancient Israel: Japhethites in the north (Anatolia, Indo-European peoples), Hamites in the south (Egypt, Africa, Canaan), Shemites in the middle east (Mesopotamia, Arabia). The seventy-nations tradition becomes foundational — Jesus sends out seventy disciples in Luke 10:1, signaling a mission to the totality of nations.",
        "User original",
    ),
