    (
        10,
        11,
        "",
        "into Assyria, and built Nineveh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Assyria’s beginnings.</strong> Genesis traces the founding of both Babylonian and Assyrian civilizations to Nimrod — the two empires that would later devastate Israel and Judah respectively. The literary compression links them as twin imperial powers tracing to the same Hamitic line. Nineveh becomes the great enemy in the prophetic literature (Jonah, Nahum), making Genesis 10’s brief notice proleptic. The genealogy thus quietly establishes that Israel’s later imperial threats share a common ancestry — a narrative theme that informs prophetic theology of judgment on the nations.",
        "User original",
    ),
