    (
        10,
        25,
        "p",
        "in his days the earth was divided",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Peleg — what division?",
        '<strong>What division? Three readings.</strong> The phrase <em>niflegah ha-aretz</em> (“the earth was divided”) in Peleg’s day has been read three ways: (1) <em>linguistic division</em> — the Babel scattering of Gen 11:1–9, with Peleg’s lifespan synchronizing with the dispersal; this is the consensus rabbinic and traditional Christian reading; (2) <em>tribal/territorial allocation</em> — the division of lands among the post-flood peoples per <a href="index_split_019.html#ch-b15-c8">Jubilees 8–9</a>, where Noah parcels out the earth by lot to his sons (a tradition unique to this canon); (3) <em>geological/continental</em> — a literalist modern reading positing continental drift in Peleg’s lifetime, untenable on geological timescales. The Jubilees reading is exegetically richest: it provides the missing transition between Gen 10 (peoples) and Gen 11 (scattering).',
        "User paraphrase; references Jubilees",
    ),
