        "p",
        "let’s make a name for ourselves",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "shem — the irony",
        "<strong><em>Shem</em> (שֵׁם) — the irony of the name.</strong> The Babel-builders say <em>na‘aseh-lanu shem</em> (“let us make ourselves a name/<em>shem</em>”) — and the very next paragraph (11:10) launches the genealogy of <em>Shem</em>, the actual line of name and blessing. The wordplay is structural: those who try to make their own name are scattered; the name that endures is given by God through descent. The Babel project is the inversion of Abrahamic election (12:2 — “I will make your name great”): the builders try to make a name through self-elevation; Abram receives a great name through divine call and obedience. The vocabulary is identical; the direction is opposite.",
        "User original",
