        "Yahweh came down to see",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Divine descent.</strong> The image of God “coming down” recurs in Scripture: Sinai (Ex 19:11), Sodom (Gen 18:21), and at the Incarnation (John 1:14). The anthropomorphic descent serves a rhetorical-irony function — God who is everywhere comes down because the tower-builders aspired to ascend. The phrase “to see” echoes 18:21 (“I will go down and see”) — divine “investigation” as narrative device emphasizing divine concern with human action. The Targum tradition softens to “the Word of Yahweh was revealed,” removing the embodied descent.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum",
