        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Therefore is the name of it called Babel; because the LORD did there confound the language of all the earth&#x27; Ephrem reads as the etymological-theological hinge that interprets the entire Old Testament&#x27;s subsequent encounter with multiple languages. The confusion of tongues (the Hebrew balal = to mix / confound) at Babel is divine judgment-as-mercy: the unified human project was unifiedly evil; fragmentation by language limits the scope of any single evil. The Pentecost (Acts 2) Ephrem reads as the inverse-recapitulation: not the abolition of linguistic diversity but the Spirit&#x27;s gift of cross-linguistic intelligibility around the proclamation of Christ. The Tewahedo Pentecost liturgy explicitly cross-references Gen 11:9 in its hymns, preserving this Ephremic Babel-Pentecost typological pair.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis XI.9 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
