    (
        11,
        9,
        "p",
        "its name was called Babel",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "balal / babel — the wordplay",
        '<strong><em>Balal</em> / <em>Babel</em> — wordplay.</strong> The Hebrew <em>balal</em> (“to confuse, mix up”) is paronomastic with <em>Babel</em>. The Babylonians themselves called their city <em>Bāb-ilim</em> (Akkadian) — “gate of god” — a self-aggrandizing etymology. The Genesis text deliberately subverts this: where Babylonian self-understanding heard “gate of God,” the Hebrew narrator hears “confusion.” It is a polemical re-naming, similar to the prophetic rhetoric in <a href="index_split_040.html#ch-b37-c46">Isaiah 46:1</a> and Jeremiah 50–51. The wordplay sets up the long biblical career of Babylon as the archetypal opposing kingdom — Babylon the destroyer of Jerusalem (2 Kgs 25), Babylon the place of exile (Ps 137), Babylon the Great in <a href="index_split_060.html#ch-b86-c17">Revelation 17–18</a>.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        11,
        9,
        "q",
        "its name was called Babel",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Pentecost as Babel reversed",
        '<strong>Pentecost as Babel reversed.</strong> <a href="index_split_055.html#ch-b64-c2">Acts 2:6–11</a> consciously inverts Babel: at Babel, one language is divided into many (judgment); at Pentecost, many languages hear one gospel (grace). The narrative structures mirror each other — both feature a multinational gathering, a verbal phenomenon, and a redistributive outcome. But where Babel disperses by confusion, Pentecost gathers by clarity. The Spirit does not reverse the linguistic diversity (each hears in his own tongue); rather, the Spirit makes diversity hospitable to a single message. The eschatological vision in <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b86-c7">Revelation 7:9</a> — “every nation, tribe, people, and tongue” praising the Lamb — completes the trajectory: Babel’s scattering is finally redeemed not by re-unification but by harmonic plurality.',
        "User original",
    ),
