    (
        11,
        30,
        "",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The barren matriarch motif begins here.</strong> Sarai’s barrenness opens a pattern repeated by Rebekah, Rachel, Hannah, and Elizabeth: the promised line repeatedly comes through wombs that should not bear. The motif sets up the theological grammar of grace — the next chapter (Gen 12) will hinge on God promising offspring to a couple who, by every natural reckoning, can have none. In rabbinic readings (b. Yevamot 64a), the motif demonstrates that the patriarchs and matriarchs needed prayer to conceive — the line of promise is sustained by petition, not biology.",
        "User original",
    ),
