    (
        11,
        31,
        "p",
        "They went from Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Canaan",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Terah’s incomplete journey",
        '<strong>Terah’s incomplete journey.</strong> The text says Terah set out from Ur with the intention of reaching Canaan, but stopped at Haran and lived there until his death (11:32). The journey to Canaan is begun by the father and completed by the son — the same pattern that will recur with Moses (who sees the land but does not enter) and Joshua (who completes the entry). Stephen’s speech in <a href="index_split_055.html#ch-b64-c7">Acts 7:2–4</a> claims that God appeared to Abram “before he dwelt in Haran,” suggesting that the divine call originated in Ur and that Abram’s journey is the completion of a vocation his father only partially answered. The narrative tension between 11:31 (Terah’s journey) and 12:1 (Abram’s call) is left unresolved — the patriarchal line is launched on partial obedience and inherited promise.',
        "User original",
    ),
