    (
        12,
        10,
        "",
        "Abram went down into Egypt",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The first Egyptian sojourn.</strong> Abram’s descent to Egypt to escape Canaanite famine establishes a pattern that will recur structurally throughout Genesis and Exodus: famine drives the patriarchal family to Egypt; Egyptian sojourn carries dangers (here, Pharaoh takes Sarai into his harem); divine intervention restores the family to Canaan with augmented wealth (12:16, 20). The pattern recurs almost identically with Isaac (Gen 26, except restricted to Gerar by Yahweh’s explicit prohibition of Egypt) and culminates in Jacob’s descent in chs 46–47, which leads ultimately to Egyptian slavery and the exodus. The chapter functions as a small-scale prefiguration of the entire exodus arc: Sarai threatened in Pharaoh’s house (foreshadowing Israel threatened in Egypt); plagues afflict Pharaoh’s house (12:17, "great plagues"); the family is expelled (12:20, "they sent him away") with Egyptian wealth (12:16). The reader who knows Exodus recognizes the prefigurement: Abram’s small-scale Egyptian episode is the type of Israel’s large-scale Egyptian story.',
        "User original",
    ),
