        "as the dust of the earth",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Innumerable as dust.</strong> The first of three patriarchal innumerability metaphors — dust here, stars at 15:5, sand at 22:17. Each evokes a different dimension of immensity: dust (omnipresent grounding), stars (cosmic scope), sand (boundary against chaos). The dust metaphor specifically resonates with Genesis 2:7 — humanity made from dust — making Abrahamic offspring participants in renewed humanity. Rabbinic exegesis (<em>Bereshit Rabbah</em> 41:9) found in the three metaphors the threefold structure of Israel’s destiny.",
