    (
        16,
        2,
        "",
        "It may be that I will obtain children by her",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Surrogacy in ANE legal context.</strong> Sarai’s plan is not unprecedented — the Code of Hammurabi (§§144–146) and the Nuzi tablets attest to the practice of barren wives providing handmaids as concubines, with the resulting children counted to the wife’s account. The legal framework makes Sarai’s plan legitimate under contemporary norms. The narrative judgment, however, is implicit but unmistakable: every detail of the resulting story is fraught with conflict and divine displeasure. The text models the canonical-legal tension between social custom and theological vocation.",
        "User paraphrase; references Code of Hammurabi",
    ),
