    (
        16,
        7,
        "",
        "Yahweh’s angel found her",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The first angel-appearance.</strong> The "angel of Yahweh" (Hebrew <em>mal’akh Adonai</em>) appears here for the first time in Genesis — and to Hagar, the Egyptian slave-woman fleeing Sarah’s mistreatment, not to Abraham or Sarah. The episode is theologically remarkable: the first divine messenger encounter goes to a non-Israelite female slave, not to the patriarchal-line. Hagar will subsequently name Yahweh "El-roi" — "the God who sees me" (16:13) — making her the only person in the OT to give a name to God. The chapter is also one of Genesis’s clearest instances of the inner-canonical reversal of expectation: divine attention privileges the marginalized over the established. Phyllis Trible’s reading in <em>Texts of Terror</em> takes Hagar’s narrative as one of the OT’s clearest examples of theological literature giving voice and dignity to the structurally voiceless. The verse opens one of the patriarchal cycle’s most morally and theologically complex sub-narratives.',
        "User original",
    ),
