    (
        17,
        10,
        "",
        "Every male among you shall be circumcised",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The covenant of circumcision.</strong> The command institutes the practice that becomes the OT’s most distinctive single physical marker of covenant identity. Circumcision was practiced in many ANE cultures (Egypt, the Levant, parts of Mesopotamia) but typically as a puberty-rite or marriage-preparation; Israelite uniqueness is the eighth-day-after-birth timing (17:12) and the explicit theological framing as covenant sign rather than social-developmental rite. The practice becomes definitive of Jewish identity, contested in NT theology (Acts 15, the Jerusalem Council; Galatians on Paul’s apostolic-era debate over Gentile circumcision), and re-spiritualized as "circumcision of the heart" (Deut 10:16, 30:6, Jer 4:4, Rom 2:28–29). The verse is one of the OT’s most institutionally durable provisions; circumcision continues as primary Jewish identity-marker into the present. The chapter as a whole is also Abram’s name-change to Abraham, marking the covenant moment with both somatic (circumcision) and onomastic (renaming) signs.',
        "User original",
    ),
