        "cut off from his people",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong><em>Karet</em> — the cutting off.</strong> The Hebrew penalty <em>karet</em> (<em>כָּרֵת</em>, “cutting off”) is variously interpreted: divine punishment by premature death (rabbinic majority view), exclusion from the people of God, or exclusion from the world to come (m. Sanhedrin 10). The verb-noun resonance is striking: failure to “cut” the foreskin results in being “cut off.” The penalty for omission of the sign is the very thing the sign was meant to enact — incorporation into the covenant people. <em>Karet</em> appears as the penalty for over twenty serious offenses in the Pentateuch.",
        "User original",
