        '<strong>The three visitors.</strong> The text alternates between singular ("Yahweh appeared," 18:1) and plural ("three men"). Three classical readings: (1) Yahweh accompanied by two angels (the two who proceed to Sodom in 19:1), (2) a triadic theophany read by Christian tradition (especially Eastern Orthodox, hence Rublev’s famous icon) as a foreshadowing of Trinity, (3) a single divine appearance through angelic mediation. The text is deliberately ambiguous — the boundary between Yahweh and his messenger is repeatedly blurred across Genesis.',
        "User original",
