        "and they ate",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Divine eating.</strong> The “men” eat the meal Abraham prepares — a startling anthropomorphism if read as Yahweh literally consuming food. Targum Onkelos paraphrases as “they appeared to eat,” softening the embodied act. Patristic interpretation (Justin Martyr, Tertullian) read the passage as evidence of divine corporeality before the incarnation; later orthodox tradition (Augustine) treated it as a theophanic accommodation. The detail is theologically pregnant: divine immanence reaches the point of shared meal — table fellowship that anticipates the eucharistic meal.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum, Tertullian, Augustine",
