    (
        18,
        14,
        "",
        "Is anything too hard for Yahweh?",
        "comm-orthodox",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The rhetorical question.</strong> The visitor’s question (Hebrew <em>ha-yiplah me-Adonai davar</em>) becomes one of the OT’s most-quoted single lines in subsequent biblical literature: Jer 32:17, 27 ("nothing is too hard for you"), Zech 8:6, Luke 1:37 (the angel’s assurance to Mary, "with God nothing is impossible," in nearly direct allusion to this verse). The question is rhetorical only because the answer is presupposed; it functions theologically as a foundational claim about divine capability. The verse’s literary placement is significant: Sarah has just laughed at the announcement that she will conceive at age 90 (18:12), and the question both rebukes her skepticism and invites her into trust. The chapter’s entire scene — three visitors, hospitality at Mamre, the announcement of Isaac’s conception — has had unusual interpretive history: Christian tradition (especially Eastern Orthodox icons of the "Hospitality of Abraham") has read the three visitors as Trinitarian theophany, with Andrei Rublev’s 15th-century icon making this verse-cluster one of the most-depicted scenes in iconographic tradition.',
        "User original",
    ),
