        "",
        "the fear of God is not in this place",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The fear of God.</strong> Abraham’s defense — he assumed Gerar lacked the fear of God — was wrong about Abimelech specifically (the king proved more morally upright than Abraham). The phrase <em>yirʾat Elohim</em> (“fear of God”) functions in the Hebrew Bible as a near-equivalent of moral integrity (Job 1:1; Eccl 12:13). The irony of the chapter is that Abraham, the man of faith, proves a worse moral actor than Abimelech, the pagan king. The narrative refuses any easy correlation between covenant identity and moral superiority.",
        "User original",
