        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Yitzḥaq (<em>יִצְחָק</em>) — "he laughs."</strong> Sarah’s play on Isaac’s name closes a thread of laughter running through the Abraham cycle: Abraham laughed at the promise (17:17), Sarah laughed at the messengers (18:12), and now both laugh in joy. The Hebrew root <em>tz-ḥ-q</em> threads three different laughs — incredulous, derisive, joyful — into the patriarchal narrative.',
