        33,
        "",
        "tamarisk tree in Beersheba",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The tamarisk and the well.</strong> Abraham plants a tamarisk tree at Beersheba and "called there on the name of Yahweh, the Everlasting God" (Hebrew <em>El Olam</em>). The detail is one of Genesis’s small archaeological-cultic windows: tree-planting at well-sites was a recognized form of patriarchal-period sacred-space marking, with the tree functioning as visible long-term identifier of the encounter. The same pattern recurs elsewhere (the oak of Mamre, the oak of Moreh) — patriarchal "altars" in the loose sense were often actually trees, wells, or stones, marking sites where divine encounter had occurred. The "Everlasting God" name is theologically significant: <em>El Olam</em> identifies Yahweh with permanence-eternity beyond the patriarchal family’s ephemeral existence. The chapter as a whole is also significant for the well-rights treaty between Abraham and Abimelech (21:22–32) — one of Genesis’s clearest portraits of patriarchal-era diplomacy in the Negev. The tamarisk and the well together encode a recurring patriarchal-period cultic-political pattern.',
        "User original",
