    (
        22,
        13,
        "",
        "a ram caught in the thicket",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The ram substitute.</strong> The substitution — ram for Isaac — establishes the foundational logic of substitutionary atonement. The animal “caught in the thicket” appears at the precise moment of need, providentially rather than accidentally. The Hebrew text actually reads <em>ʾaḥar</em> (“after, behind”), prompting some traditional commentators to find the ram coming from behind — as if substitution arrives at the moment of decision. Christian theology has read the ram as type of Christ; the lamb provided by God is the ultimate fulfillment of Abraham’s prophetic claim at 22:8 (“God will provide himself the lamb”).",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        22,
        13,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: ABRAHAM, ABRAHAM, ANER, COURAGE, FAITH. <em>[Reviewer: pick one topic (typically the first or most theologically loaded), write a 2–3 sentence thematic note, and discard the rest.]</em>",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        22,
        14,
        "",
        "Yahweh Will Provide",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Yahweh-Yireh.</strong> The site-name <em>Adonai Yireh</em> ("Yahweh sees" or "Yahweh provides," Hebrew <em>r-’-h</em> functioning ambiguously) commemorates the substitution of the ram for Isaac. The Hebrew root pun runs through the chapter — Abraham earlier said God would "see/provide" (<em>yireh</em>, 22:8) the lamb; the moment of provision becomes the place-name. The mountain is identified in 2 Chr 3:1 as Mount Moriah, the eventual temple-mount; rabbinic tradition (with strong textual support) reads the Akedah (the binding of Isaac) as foundational sacrifice-site for the entire Jerusalem cult. The chapter as a whole is one of the OT’s most theologically demanding texts — Kierkegaard’s <em>Fear and Trembling</em>, Erich Auerbach’s <em>Mimesis</em>, and Levinas’s readings of the Akedah have made the chapter central to modern philosophy of religion. The verse establishes the foundational principle: Yahweh provides at the moment of impossibility. The principle becomes one of the OT’s structuring theological premises — provision arrives precisely at the point at which human resource has been exhausted.',
        "User original",
    ),
