    (
        24,
        3,
        "",
        "not take a wife for my son of the daughters of the Canaanites",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Endogamy and covenant boundary.</strong> Abraham’s prohibition on Canaanite intermarriage establishes a pattern that becomes legally formalized at Deuteronomy 7:3 (no marriage with Canaanites). The reasoning is theological, not ethnic: Canaanites would draw Israel into idolatry (Deut 7:4). The principle is reaffirmed by Ezra-Nehemiah (Ezra 9–10; Neh 13:23–27) post-exile, when intermarriage threatens covenant identity. Yet the Bible also includes positive non-Israelite marriages (Ruth, Tamar) — making the principle context-specific rather than absolute.",
        "User original",
    ),
