        "because she was barren",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The barrenness motif continued.</strong> Rebekah is the second matriarch to be barren (after Sarah). The pattern continues with Rachel (29:31), and outside Genesis with Hannah (1 Sam 1) and Elizabeth (Luke 1). Each barrenness narrative establishes that the chosen offspring is divine gift, not biological certainty. The repetition is theologically significant: covenant continuation is always at risk and always supplied by divine intervention. Isaac’s entreaty (Hebrew <em>vayeʿtar</em>) is a strong intercessory term — Isaac actively prays, modeling priestly mediation.",
        "User original",
