        27,
        38,
        "",
        "Do you have just one blessing",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>One blessing only?</strong> Esau’s question reveals the patriarchal blessing’s ontology: a definite, finite act of speech rather than an indefinitely renewable wish. Once Jacob received the blessing of headship and dominion, Isaac had no comparable blessing to give Esau. The lesser blessing he eventually gives (27:39–40) is not equivalent. The economy of blessing — finite, transferable, irrevocable — undergirds the firstborn-displacement pattern in Genesis (Cain/Abel/Seth, Ishmael/Isaac, Esau/Jacob, Manasseh/Ephraim). The chosen line is not the natural firstborn but the one who receives the irreplaceable word.",
        "User original",
