        "",
        "she named him Reuben",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Reuben’s name.</strong> Reuben’s name (<em>Reʾuven</em>, <em>רְאוּבֵן</em>) word-plays on “see, a son” (<em>reʾu ben</em>) and also on Yahweh’s “seeing” (<em>raʾah</em>) Leah’s affliction. Each of Leah’s sons receives a name connected to her marital pain — the names trace her hope of winning Jacob’s love. Reuben (“see, a son”), Simeon (“heard”), Levi (“joined”), Judah (“I will praise”). The naming patterns reveal the underlying matriarchal subjectivity often eclipsed in patriarchal narrative — the women name the children, encoding their experience into Israel’s tribal identities.",
        "User original",
