    (
        31,
        19,
        "",
        "Rachel stole the teraphim",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The teraphim theft.</strong> Rachel’s theft of her father’s household idols is one of the OT’s most discussed single details. The teraphim (small household figurines, probably ancestral) likely had legal-inheritance significance in Mesopotamian household practice — the bearer of the teraphim could claim primary inheritance rights, attested in Nuzi tablets. Rachel’s theft therefore may have functioned as a final claim on Laban’s household property (cf. 31:14, where Rachel and Leah complain that Laban has treated them as foreigners and withheld their inheritance). The theft’s narrative consequence is significant: when Laban catches up and searches the camp, Rachel hides the teraphim under a camel-saddle and refuses to rise "because the way of women is on me" (31:34–35) — using menstruation-impurity to keep her father from approaching the saddle. The episode is one of Genesis’s most layered: female agency, household-religion, inheritance-claim, and ritual-impurity all combine in a single brief sequence. Most strikingly, it shows household-idol religion within the patriarchal-line family — a messy reality the narrator records without comment.',
        "User original",
    ),
