    (
        31,
        49,
        "",
        "Yahweh watch between me and you",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Mizpah benediction.</strong> Laban’s closing blessing at Mizpah ("the Lord watch between me and you, when we are absent one from another") has had perhaps the longest devotional afterlife of any single Genesis verse, used as a parting blessing in countless Christian liturgies and as the textual root of the modern "Mizpah" jewelry tradition (broken-heart pendants exchanged at separations). The verse’s narrative context complicates its devotional use significantly: Laban’s "blessing" is in fact a wary boundary-treaty, more covenant of mutual deterrence ("if you afflict my daughters, or take other wives… God is witness," 31:50) than warm valedictory wish. The two parties have just spent twenty years deceiving each other; the Mizpah pillar marks the territorial line they will not cross. The verse’s history of devotional use is one of the OT’s clearest examples of a phrase outliving its narrative context entirely — its weight in modern Christian piety has nothing to do with what it actually means in Genesis 31. The chapter is also significant for naming the place "Galeed" (Hebrew "heap of witness") and "Mizpah" ("watchtower"), two of Genesis’s many name-etiologies.',
        "User original",
    ),
