    (
        32,
        28,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Thy name shall be called no more Jacob, but Israel: for as a prince hast thou power with God and with men, and hast prevailed&#x27;. Ephrem reads the new name as won-not-given: unlike Abraham&#x27;s name change (simple divine declaration), Jacob WRESTLES for his new name. Tewahedo monastic-ascetical tradition uses this verse as the canonical warrant for spiritual struggle: some names (i.e., spiritual identities) are not handed over but must be wrested through prayer-as-combat. The monastic name received at tonsure is in this lineage.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXII.28. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
