        33,
        10,
        "",
        "as one sees the face of God",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Esau’s face as God’s face.</strong> Jacob’s startling claim — “I have seen your face, as one sees the face of God” — pairs directly with Peniel (32:30, “I have seen God face to face”). The doubled use of the face-of-God motif theologizes the human encounter: Jacob’s wrestling with God prepared him to see God in his brother’s face. Levinas’s philosophical theology of “the face of the other” draws explicitly on this Genesis tradition — the human face is the locus of ethical encounter and irreducibly other. The reconciliation with Esau is theologically continuous with the wrestling at Jabbok.",
        "User original",
