    (
        34,
        2,
        "",
        "lay with her, and humbled her",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The rape of Dinah.</strong> The chapter opens with one of the OT’s starkest portrayals of sexual violence: Shechem, the prince of the local city, takes Dinah and "humbles her" (Hebrew <em>vay’anneha</em>, the same verb used of sexual violation throughout OT law — Deut 22:24, 29). The narrative complications begin immediately: Shechem then "loved the young woman" (34:3) and proposes marriage. The text refuses easy readings — what begins as violation transitions into proposed alliance, and Dinah herself never speaks. Phyllis Trible’s and Mieke Bal’s feminist readings have made this chapter central to OT scholarship on gendered violence and silence. The brothers’ vengeance — Simeon and Levi’s deceitful slaughter of all the Shechemite men, while convalescent from circumcision — Jacob deplores but does not retract; his deathbed curse on Simeon and Levi (49:5–7) traces directly to this episode. The chapter encodes one of the OT’s most morally unresolved single narratives.',
        "User original",
    ),
