    (
        34,
        13,
        "",
        "because he had defiled Dinah their sister",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Deceit answers violation.</strong> The brothers’ deceitful response — agreeing to circumcision in apparent acceptance — uses the covenant sign as a weapon. The chapter’s moral structure is layered: Shechem’s violation, the brothers’ deception of the Shechemites, the city’s mass murder. The narrator describes but does not explicitly judge — leaving the moral evaluation to Jacob’s later denunciation (49:5–7, “Simeon and Levi are brothers; weapons of violence are their swords”). Critics have read the chapter as theologically pessimistic: covenant practices can be weaponized, and the response to evil produces further evil.",
        "User original",
    ),
