    (
        34,
        30,
        "",
        "You have troubled me",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Jacob’s realpolitik.</strong> Jacob’s rebuke of Simeon and Levi after their slaughter at Shechem is striking for what it does not say: there is no moral censure of the killing itself, only practical complaint that the violence has made the family politically vulnerable ("I shall be destroyed, I and my house"). The brothers’ retort — "Should he treat our sister as a prostitute?" (34:31) — defends honor; Jacob defends survival. The narrative leaves the moral evaluation to the reader and to Jacob’s deathbed (49:5–7), where Jacob finally pronounces the moral judgment ("cursed be their anger… I will scatter them in Jacob"). The delayed verdict is a Genesis literary signature: moral judgments often arrive chapters or generations after the events, distributed across the canon (Simeon’s eventual absorption into Judah, Lev’s scattering across the tribes). The chapter establishes the principle: actions have consequences that take time to ripen. The Shechem episode’s long aftermath runs through the rest of the patriarchal narrative.',
        "User original",
    ),
