        35,
        2,
        "",
        "Put away the foreign gods",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Putting away foreign gods.</strong> Jacob commands his household to dispose of foreign gods — the first explicit Israelite gesture of religious purification. The <em>elohei nekhar</em> (“foreign gods”) likely include Rachel’s stolen teraphim (31:19) and other household deities. The command is paired with ritual ablution (“purify yourselves”) and changing of garments — the elements that later structure pilgrimage to the temple. The pattern recurs at Joshua 24:14–15 (“put away the gods that your fathers served”) and 1 Samuel 7:3 (Samuel’s call). Jacob’s command anticipates the Mosaic prohibitions while predating them.",
        "User original",
