    (
        37,
        35,
        "",
        "I will go down to Sheol to my son",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Sheol — the first occurrence.</strong> Jacob’s lament contains the first occurrence of <em>Sheʾol</em> (<em>שְׁאוֹל</em>) in the Bible — the underworld realm of the dead. Sheol is morally undifferentiated in the early Hebrew Bible — both righteous and wicked descend there. The development from Sheol into the bipartite afterlife (Paradise + Gehenna) emerges only later, especially in Second Temple Judaism. Jacob’s invocation of Sheol-reunion (“I will go down to my son”) hints at conceptions of postmortem family reunion that exceed the technical theology of Sheol — a tension between official doctrine and lived hope that recurs throughout the Bible.",
        "User original",
    ),
