    (
        38,
        8,
        "",
        "perform the duty of a husband’s brother",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Levirate marriage.</strong> The institution that requires a brother to marry his deceased brother’s childless widow to "raise up offspring" for the dead. Codified in Deut 25:5–10. The Tamar / Onan / Judah narrative (Gen 38) is the Bible’s first extended case study; the book of Ruth is the second. Onan’s "spilling on the ground" is what the modern English word <em>onanism</em> derives from — though the text’s concern is the breach of levirate duty, not contraception per se.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        38,
        8,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: ADULTERY, INHERITANCE, MARRIAGE, ONAN, TAMAR. <em>[Reviewer: pick one topic (typically the first or most theologically loaded), write a 2–3 sentence thematic note, and discard the rest.]</em>",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        38,
        8,
        "b",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>LEVIRATE LAW</strong> from Latin levir, “a husband’s brother,” the name of an ancient custom ordained by Moses, by which, when an Israelite died without issue, his surviving brother was required to marry the widow, so as to continue his brother’s family through the son that might be born of that marriage (Genesis 38:8; Deuteronomy 25:5-10; comp. Ruth 3; 4:10). Its object was “to raise up seed to the departed brother.” <em>[Reviewer: condense Easton's entry as needed.]</em>",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        38,
        9,
        "",
        "spilled his semen on the ground",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Onan’s act.</strong> The verse becomes the source for the term “onanism,” later used (especially in Catholic moral theology) to denote masturbation or non-procreative sexual acts. The original biblical issue, however, is specifically refusal to fulfill the levirate duty (raising offspring for a deceased brother) — an economic-genealogical refusal rather than the act of seed-spilling per se. The text’s “evil in Yahweh’s sight” (38:10) attaches moral weight to the refusal. Modern Catholic moral theology has continued the prohibition on contraception by analogy; Protestant and Jewish traditions generally read the offense more narrowly as levirate-refusal.",
        "User original",
    ),
