    (
        39,
        9,
        "",
        "How then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Joseph’s refusal.</strong> The verse contains the OT’s most concentrated single articulation of moral integrity under sexual temptation. Joseph’s reasoning is theologically precise: the wrong is not primarily against Potiphar (though Potiphar’s trust would be betrayed), nor against Potiphar’s wife (though she would be implicated in transgression), but "against God." The episode is one of the OT’s clearest demonstrations that ethics is grounded vertically in covenantal relationship before it is grounded horizontally in social obligation. The detail is also narratively significant: Joseph refuses what would be (in ANE court culture) ordinary opportunistic advancement — a powerful patron’s wife’s favor — and pays for the refusal with imprisonment. The chapter’s subsequent vindication theology (39:21–23, Yahweh’s favor in prison) makes the moral framework clear: integrity costs short-term, but Yahweh’s providence eventually reverses the apparent loss. Joseph’s rise to the second highest position in Egypt comes through, not despite, this initial integrity. The episode is one of the OT’s clearest Wisdom-narrative anticipations of the Proverbs theme of righteous suffering vindicated.',
        "User original",
    ),
