        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;There is none greater in this house than I; neither hath he kept back any thing from me but thee, because thou art his wife: how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against God?&#x27; Ephrem reads Joseph&#x27;s reasoning as a perfect model of conscience: he refuses on FAITHFULNESS-to-Potiphar AND on faithfulness-to-God. Tewahedo monastic-ethical tradition cites Joseph as the canonical chastity-model: the rejection is reasoned, not just instinctive.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXIX.9. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
