        39,
        12,
        "",
        "left his garment in her hand",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The left garment.</strong> Joseph’s flight, leaving his garment behind, is the third time clothing serves as evidence in the Joseph narrative: (1) his bloodied tunic at 37:31–33; (2) here; (3) Pharaoh’s signet-and-robe at 41:42 in his eventual elevation. The pattern of identity-clothing recurs throughout Joseph’s story — clothing as markers of social status that are repeatedly stripped and given. Theologians have read the motif as type of Christ stripped of garments at crucifixion (John 19:23–24) and clothed in white at resurrection. Joseph repeatedly loses clothes only to receive better ones.",
        "User original",
