        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Do not interpretations belong to God? tell me them, I pray you&#x27;. Ephrem treats Joseph&#x27;s reply to the cupbearer and baker as foundational for the doctrine of divine charism: interpretation of dreams is not a human skill but a divine gift, channelled through specific persons. Tewahedo prophetic-discernment tradition (the monastic discernment-of-spirits charism) traces theologically to this verse: the discerner DOES the interpreting but the interpretation BELONGS to God.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XL.8. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
