        40,
        13,
        "",
        "lift up your head",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Two “lift up your head” idioms.</strong> The Hebrew idiom “lift up the head” (<em>nasa rosh</em>) carries opposite meanings in 40:13 (cup-bearer restored) and 40:19 (baker beheaded). The same words generate diametrically opposite outcomes — restoration versus decapitation. The wordplay is deliberate, demonstrating Joseph’s interpretive precision. Numbers 1:2’s military census uses the same idiom for “take a count of the head” — counting and beheading share vocabulary. The rhetorical tension between identical phrases pointing to opposite fates becomes a hallmark of biblical wisdom literature.",
        "User original",
