        "birds will eat your flesh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The unburied body.</strong> The baker’s fate — body unburied, devoured by birds — was the most severe ANE post-mortem disgrace. Burial was understood as essential for proper afterlife; unburied corpses indicated the deceased’s exclusion from family-gathering. The same fate is pronounced on Jezebel (2 Kings 9:36–37). The motif also resonates with Egyptian beliefs about preservation of the body for afterlife — making the baker’s fate especially horrific in his cultural context. Joseph’s interpretation does not soften the message; he tells the truth even when it means death.",
