        "the chief cup bearer didn’t remember Joseph",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The forgotten Joseph.</strong> The chapter ends with bitter irony: Joseph is forgotten by the very man whose life he interpreted. The waiting period — “two full years” (41:1) — is theologically central. Joseph’s elevation comes not at human petition but at divine timing through Pharaoh’s own dreams. The pattern teaches biblical providence: human cooperation may be expected and refused, yet the larger plan unfolds without dependence on human reliability. The motif of being forgotten and later remembered structures Israel’s exilic experience — God remembers when humans forget.",
        "User original",
