        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Pharaoh said unto his servants, Can we find such a one as this is, a man in whom the Spirit of God is?&#x27; Ephrem reads Pharaoh&#x27;s recognition as a striking example: a pagan king discerns the Spirit of God in a Hebrew slave. Tewahedo missiological theology preserves this verse as the canonical warrant for the principle that divine presence can be recognised by those outside the covenant — implicit support for the Tewahedo openness to natural-revelation theology.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XLI.38. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
