        "Note",
        '<strong>M’nasheh (<em>מְנַשֶּׁה</em>) — from <em>nashah</em>, "to forget."</strong> Joseph names his firstborn for the forgetting of his father’s house — bittersweet. Compare Ephraim (v.52, <em>parah</em>, "to be fruitful"). The two Egyptian-born sons, given Hebrew names, will become the dominant northern tribes after the conquest; "Joseph" as a tribal designation effectively splits in two through them.',
