        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>HADES</strong> that which is out of sight, a Greek word used to denote the state or place of the dead. All the dead alike go into this place. To be buried, to go down to the grave, to descend into hades, are equivalent expressions. In the LXX. this word is the usual rendering of the Hebrew sheol, the common receptacle of the departed (Genesis 42:38; Psalm 139:8; Hos. 13:14; Isaiah 14:9). This term is of comparatively rare occurrence in the Greek New Testament. Our Lord speaks of Capernaum… <em>[Reviewer: condense Easton's entry as needed.]</em>",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
