        26,
        "",
        "bowed themselves down to the earth before him",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The brothers bow again.</strong> The second prostration — this time including Benjamin — fulfills the second part of Joseph’s sheaves dream more completely. The text seems to deliberately repeat the language of 42:6 (“bowed themselves down”), creating a doubled fulfillment that parallels the doubled dream itself. Joseph’s two dreams (sheaves, then sun-moon-stars) had different scopes; the dream-fulfillments unfold across multiple bowings. The narrative structure teaches that prophetic fulfillment can be staged across time — not all at once but in cumulative scenes.",
        "User original",
