    (
        45,
        7,
        "",
        "preserve for you a remnant",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Preserving a remnant.</strong> Joseph’s reading of his Egyptian elevation as preservation of a “remnant” (<em>sheʾerit</em>, <em>שְׁאֵרִית</em>) introduces remnant theology into the patriarchal narrative. The same vocabulary structures the prophetic traditions (Isa 10:21–22 “a remnant will return”; Mic 4:7; Zech 8:6). The remnant concept becomes central to Pauline theology of Israel (Rom 9–11). Joseph’s exile, far from the line’s destruction, is its preservation — a paradoxical pattern that informs all biblical exile theology. The remnant is preserved by going down before going up.",
        "User original",
    ),
