    (
        46,
        4,
        "",
        "I will also surely bring you up again",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Exodus promise.</strong> God’s promise to Jacob — “I will surely bring you up again” — anticipates the entire Exodus narrative four centuries before it begins. The verb <em>aʿaleh</em> (“I will bring up”) becomes the standard verb for the Exodus event. The Israel-in-Egypt narrative is here framed not as exile but as preservation-with-promised-return. The verse anchors the eventual Exodus in patriarchal divine speech rather than emerging spontaneously at Moses’s call. The continuity of covenant promise across the long Egyptian sojourn is theologically central: God’s word at the descent already contains the ascent.",
        "User original",
    ),
