        "Let them dwell in the land of Goshen",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Goshen.</strong> Goshen, in the eastern Nile Delta, becomes the geographic stage for Israel’s entire Egyptian sojourn (Exodus 8:22, 9:26 specify it as the land where Israelites lived during the plagues). Modern archaeology associates Goshen with the area around Tell el-Dabʿa / Avaris — a region inhabited by Asiatic populations during the Second Intermediate Period. The narrative’s Goshen-grant fits well with the historical pattern of Asiatic settlement in Egypt’s eastern Delta. The specificity of the geographic detail anchors the patriarchal narrative in concrete archaeological geography.",
        "User original",
