        "gathered up all the money",
        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joseph’s economic centralization.</strong> Joseph’s famine-administration progressively transfers all Egyptian wealth to Pharaoh: first money, then livestock (47:16–17), then land (47:20), finally the people themselves as servants (47:21). The narrative’s apparent endorsement of this system is striking — Joseph functions as economic centralizer and effectively reduces Egypt to royal property. Modern readings have found this troubling: the same patriarchal narrative that critiques Pharaoh’s later oppression of Israel here describes Joseph’s role in establishing absolute Egyptian royal economy. Some commentators (Brueggemann) read the chapter as morally ambivalent commentary on famine-driven state power.",
        "User paraphrase; summarises Brueggemann",
