    (
        48,
        3,
        "",
        "God Almighty appeared to me at Luz",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Recalling the Bethel theophany.</strong> Jacob recalls the Bethel/Luz theophany of 28:13–15 as he prepares to bless Joseph’s sons — anchoring his patriarchal blessing in the original divine promise. The pattern of patriarchal blessings citing original divine promises becomes paradigmatic: covenant transmission requires explicit memory of foundational theophanies. Jacob’s selection of <em>El Shaddai</em> as the divine name preserves the patriarchal divine epithet rather than using Yahweh — fitting the theological scheme of Exodus 6:3 (“I appeared to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, as God Almighty; but by my name Yahweh I was not known to them”).",
        "User original",
    ),
