        "",
        "Boiling over like water",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Reuben — unstable as water.</strong> Reuben’s blessing — “unstable as water, you shall not have the preeminence” — encodes the punishment for his sleeping with Bilhah (35:22). Reuben loses the firstborn rights to Joseph (1 Chr 5:1) — the youngest’s elevation that has structured Genesis ends with the formal-poetic confirmation. The phrase <em>pachaz ka-mayim</em> (“unstable as water”) has become proverbial. The Reuben tribe never produces a major leader, judge, or king in biblical history — the prophecy’s fulfillment is unspectacular but consistent. The verse demonstrates that Genesis poetry contains not just blessings but ominous predictions of historical limitations.",
        "User original",
