        10,
        "",
        "until he comes to whom it belongs",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Shiloh.</strong> The crux of Jacob’s blessing of Judah. The Hebrew <em>shiloh</em> has been read variously: as a place name (Shiloh), as "tribute belongs to him" (<em>shai loh</em>), as "until that which is his comes" (<em>she-loh</em>). The Targums read the verse messianically and translate "until the Messiah comes." Christian tradition takes it as a Messianic prophecy fulfilled in Jesus from the line of Judah. The Dead Sea Scrolls (4QPatriarchal Blessings) read it explicitly of the messianic king. The phrase "obedience of the peoples" anticipates the universal-recognition theme of Isa 49 and elsewhere.',
        "User original",
