    (
        49,
        29,
        "",
        "Bury me with my fathers in the cave that is in the field",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The patriarchal burial.</strong> Jacob’s deathbed instruction connects the entire patriarchal narrative to its physical center: the cave of Machpelah at Hebron, purchased by Abraham in ch 23. The cave eventually receives all three patriarchs, three of the four matriarchs (Rebekah, Leah, and Sarah; Rachel was buried separately near Bethlehem, 35:19), and is held by Jewish tradition to remain the patriarchal-matriarchal burial ground today (the Tomb of the Patriarchs at Hebron, with both Jewish synagogue and Muslim mosque elements, is one of the most contested holy sites in the modern Holy Land). Jacob’s insistence on burial in Canaan — despite dying in Egypt — reflects the patriarchal theology of covenant-land: even when the family is in Egyptian residence, the burial-ground signals where they belong eschatologically. Joseph’s parallel deathbed instruction (50:25, that his bones be carried back to Canaan) is a similar gesture, eventually fulfilled in Josh 24:32. The chapter and Joseph’s parallel make burial-place itself a confession of covenant identity.",
        "User original",
    ),
