    (
        49,
        33,
        "",
        "gathered up his feet into the bed",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jacob’s death.</strong> The detail of Jacob “gathering up his feet into the bed” captures the actual physical motion of death — drawing the legs up before the final breath. The vocabulary is concrete and intimate; biblical death-scenes typically use the formulaic “gathered to his people” (here also, at the verse’s close), but this verse adds the bodily detail. The dying patriarch’s final action is to compose himself for death — agency preserved into the final moment. The same detail recurs at 1 Kings 2:1 (David’s death-preparation). Jacob’s deliberate death scene contrasts with the violent unprepared deaths that have shaped his life.",
        "User original",
    ),
