        "Note",
        "<strong>Genesis ends in Egypt.</strong> The book closes with Joseph in a coffin in Egypt — a striking ending. The patriarchal narratives that began with God’s command to Abraham to “go from your country” (12:1) end with the entire family settled in foreign land, the chosen son embalmed Egyptian-style, awaiting future return. The coffin-in-Egypt ending creates structural tension that demands continuation: the promises remain unfulfilled in the land, the people are outside it. Exodus picks up directly from this hanging note. Genesis is thus structurally incomplete — it requires Exodus, and ultimately Joshua, to complete its arc. The book of beginnings ends in promised-but-not-yet.",
